WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.215 --> 00:00:10.299
As long as people have been able to communicate, they have used codes to pass secret information to

2
00:00:10.339 --> 00:00:11.499
their friends and allies.

3
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:18.184
From the battlefields of ancient Sparta to the modern Internet, codes and ciphers have proved essential when security

4
00:00:18.224 --> 00:00:18.884
is at stake.

5
00:00:19.565 --> 00:00:26.810
Cryptography—from the Greek words kryptos, meaning "hidden," and graphos, meaning "writing"—is the art of converting a message from

6
00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:29.752
its original form into one that hides its meaning.

7
00:00:30.524 --> 00:00:36.469
The encrypted message is supposed to be understood only by its author and its intended recipient, who knows

8
00:00:36.529 --> 00:00:37.190
how to read it.

9
00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:44.396
Humans have found ingenious ways not only to create and transmit secret communications, but to intercept and decipher

10
00:00:44.416 --> 00:00:45.037
them as well.

11
00:00:45.897 --> 00:00:50.962
In order to communicate secretly, people have tried to conceal the very existence of their messages.

12
00:00:51.582 --> 00:00:57.908
For example, the ancient Greeks commonly used wax-covered wooden tablets to write on, scratching words into the wax.

13
00:00:58.496 --> 00:01:02.178
They would reuse them by simply melting new layers of wax onto the tablets.

14
00:01:02.798 --> 00:01:07.840
To keep something secret, they would write a message on the bare wood tablet and only then cover

15
00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:09.661
it with wax to make it look unused.

16
00:01:10.241 --> 00:01:15.423
When the tablet reached its destination, the recipient would melt the wax off to see the information hidden

17
00:01:15.483 --> 00:01:21.786
underneath. A Greek general relayed a message to an ally using a different technique: he shaved the head

18
00:01:21.826 --> 00:01:24.767
of a trusted slave and tattooed the information onto his head.

19
00:01:25.427 --> 00:01:30.501
After his hair grew out, the message was completely hidden, and the slave was sent to deliver it.

20
00:01:31.081 --> 00:01:37.883
Messages sent through methods like these, however, were easily intercepted if someone found out about their presence, so

21
00:01:37.943 --> 00:01:40.644
other ways to communicate in secrecy were developed.

22
00:01:41.964 --> 00:01:46.485
One of the first documented uses of a cipher was by Hebrew scribes in the ancient Middle East.

23
00:01:47.085 --> 00:01:53.107
As early as 600 BC, these scribes encrypted parts of their texts by using the "Atbash cipher," in

24
00:01:53.147 --> 00:01:57.608
which they replaced the first letter of the alphabet with the last letter, the second with the second

25
00:01:57.648 --> 00:01:58.809
to last, and so on.

26
00:01:59.529 --> 00:02:04.030
In the English version of the Atbash cipher, the letter a would instead appear as z.

27
00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:10.572
This simple process made text unreadable to anyone who did not have the key—in this case, the fact

28
00:02:10.612 --> 00:02:12.053
that the alphabet was reversed.

29
00:02:12.953 --> 00:02:17.474
By 500 BC, the Spartans of Greece were using cryptography on the battlefield.

30
00:02:18.054 --> 00:02:23.676
They passed messages with a scytale, a thin strip of leather wound tightly around a wooden stick.

31
00:02:24.388 --> 00:02:28.549
The secret message was written across the leather after it was wrapped around the stick, from one end

32
00:02:28.589 --> 00:02:29.550
of the stick to the other.

33
00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:36.172
The leather strip was then unwound, removed from the wood, and carried to its destination—as something as innocent

34
00:02:36.232 --> 00:02:37.352
as a belt, for instance.

35
00:02:38.032 --> 00:02:41.873
The recipient of the strip would then wind it around another stick of identical size.

36
00:02:42.433 --> 00:02:47.075
The letters that formed the message would line up once again, and the message could easily be read;

37
00:02:47.695 --> 00:02:50.956
if the stick was not the right size, the text would be incomprehensible.

38
00:02:52.016 --> 00:02:58.141
Several hundred years later, Julius Caesar also used a cipher during his many military campaigns to keep his

39
00:02:58.221 --> 00:02:59.121
messages secret.

40
00:02:59.742 --> 00:03:04.745
For each letter of the message, he substituted the one that appears three letters later in the alphabet.

41
00:03:05.426 --> 00:03:09.369
So the letter a would be replaced with d, b with e, and so on.

42
00:03:09.929 --> 00:03:15.193
Although the Caesar cipher is simple by today's standards, it gave Caesar a great strategic advantage.

43
00:03:15.893 --> 00:03:19.176
The Middle Ages and Renaissance saw a rapid rise in cryptography.

44
00:03:19.816 --> 00:03:26.100
The ability to communicate secretly became a priority not only of military commanders, but also of kings and

45
00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:33.824
bishops. In the fifteenth century, Leon Battista Alberti, an Italian architect, made a breakthrough in cryptography when he

46
00:03:33.864 --> 00:03:39.267
placed together two metal disks—one smaller, one larger—with the alphabet printed on each.

47
00:03:39.847 --> 00:03:44.029
The letters of the alphabet on the outer disk were in the correct order, while the letters on

48
00:03:44.049 --> 00:03:45.450
the inner circle were mixed up.

49
00:03:46.251 --> 00:03:52.315
A key indicated how to line up the disks—for instance, the message sender and its recipient would agree

50
00:03:52.355 --> 00:03:56.719
to rotate the inner disk to match its letter c with the letter a on the outer disk.

51
00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:02.484
Then, based on the pairings of the letters with the disks in that position, the message could easily

52
00:04:02.544 --> 00:04:05.086
be converted into the cipher and back again.

53
00:04:06.227 --> 00:04:12.633
Although many mechanical devices were designed for use by cryptographers, few major advances were made in cryptography until

54
00:04:12.653 --> 00:04:13.694
the nineteenth century.

55
00:04:14.495 --> 00:04:21.711
During the 1830s, Samuel Morse invented a new telegraph and Morse code, a system to encode letters into

56
00:04:21.771 --> 00:04:23.733
combinations of dots and dashes.

57
00:04:24.653 --> 00:04:29.556
This allowed messages to be tapped out and transmitted over wires in the form of short and long

58
00:04:29.596 --> 00:04:36.100
tones. By the beginning of the twentieth century, cryptography was routinely used by governments around the world.

59
00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:42.304
The telegraph machine had given way to radio, and the battle to create new methods of encryption intensified,

60
00:04:42.868 --> 00:04:47.852
because for every code invented, people known as "code breakers" worked tirelessly to solve the riddle.

61
00:04:48.593 --> 00:04:55.118
Governments created special departments to create new ciphers, as well as to intercept and decipher others' secret communications.

62
00:04:56.059 --> 00:05:01.083
As World War I loomed in Europe, the ability of nations to send and receive encrypted messages freely

63
00:05:01.123 --> 00:05:01.743
was crucial.

64
00:05:02.704 --> 00:05:08.709
Portable "cipher machines" first came into use at this time; they enabled military commanders to communicate with their

65
00:05:08.749 --> 00:05:12.172
headquarters quickly and securely, even from remote locations.

66
00:05:12.988 --> 00:05:17.030
Amid the chaos of the battlefield, the cipher machines had to be protected at all costs.

67
00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:22.992
If they fell into enemy hands, information might be revealed that would forever alter the course of events,

68
00:05:23.452 --> 00:05:24.453
as history has shown.

69
00:05:25.253 --> 00:05:30.295
During World War II, the ability to decipher encrypted communications proved instrumental.

70
00:05:30.935 --> 00:05:36.458
The United States won the critical Battle of Midway against the Japanese after breaking the code used by

71
00:05:36.518 --> 00:05:37.538
Japan's military.

72
00:05:38.318 --> 00:05:43.456
England was able to prepare for and withstand the Germans' air and submarine attacks because the British had

73
00:05:43.476 --> 00:05:46.517
broken the ciphers created by the German Enigma machine.

74
00:05:47.497 --> 00:05:53.219
The Enigma was so important that, in order to hide the fact it had been broken, England resorted

75
00:05:53.259 --> 00:05:56.960
to other explanations for its ability to resist the Germans' assaults.

76
00:05:57.881 --> 00:06:02.542
Since World War II, the science of encryption has taken a giant leap forward.

77
00:06:03.082 --> 00:06:07.704
The introduction of computers has made codes and ciphers both easier to create and to break.

78
00:06:08.404 --> 00:06:12.621
As a result, a whole industry has risen to provide for people's security needs.

79
00:06:13.362 --> 00:06:18.584
Today, cryptography is a common part of most people's daily lives, whether they know it or not.

80
00:06:19.324 --> 00:06:26.107
Whenever people enter a password on the Internet or punch a PIN (personal identification number) into an ATM,

81
00:06:26.528 --> 00:06:27.908
they are using a secret code.

82
00:06:28.829 --> 00:06:34.351
Every email message is encrypted after it leaves the computer and deciphered when it arrives at its destination.

83
00:06:36.204 --> 00:06:42.186
Hackers—people who try to break into the computer systems of governments or corporations—are actually attempting to break the

84
00:06:42.226 --> 00:06:43.766
codes used to write software.

85
00:06:44.686 --> 00:06:49.428
The desire for secrecy has been a part of human nature for thousands of years, and as humans

86
00:06:49.468 --> 00:06:52.409
have evolved, so has the art of concealing information.

87
00:06:53.169 --> 00:06:59.631
As electronic communication becomes an even greater part of our lives, new, tougher codes will be developed, and

88
00:06:59.671 --> 00:07:03.512
the importance of cryptography will continue to increase for future generations.
